# Interface shells

Document, search, and table share one structure. Follow it when adding a shell or a field.

## Sizes

- **Small**: app icon only. White 3D glyph on a solid colour tile.
- **Medium**: idle card. Same content, not yet editable.
- **Medium plus**: the card while typing. Every value on it is editable. ⌘↵ starts the chat.
- **Large**: the docked editor. Same fields, more room.

The mark beside a title is the coloured glyph with no tile, on the same line as the title.

## What is shown is editable

A value on the shell is an input, not a label. That includes targets such as `~1,500 words` and a table’s expected row count. A target stays in the same type as the caption around it, with only a light dotted underline to show it can be changed. The parts list below the library stage edits the same fields.

## Grey suggestions

Each shell shows grey secondary text for the thing a person can add, in the same voice as search’s “Add a filter”.

- Document: “Add additional context” when the document is empty. Clicking it starts a place to write.
- Search: “Add a filter”, always, with suggestions. The query is the title. Show filters only — no extra search-query examples, and no separate intent or target field.
- Table: “Add / remove columns” inside the table, and on the full table also “Add any additional rows you want”. Both sit inside the table, as rows, not underneath it.

Empty previews use that same grey sentence instead of blank bars.

## Library page

Each interface is its own row, with the size and content controls for that shell. Under the shell, three columns:

- Suggested parts, with fill with AI.
- A description of when to choose it.
- Queries: example prompts that should retrieve it. Retrieval matches a typed prompt against this list and the description.
